          paras: [
            `To understand the Bharatiya Janata Party, one must begin not in 1980 when it was formally constituted, but in 1925 when its ideological ancestor was born. BJP is the political expression of a cultural and nationalist project a century in the making. Three key institutions and two pivotal thinkers shaped this journey: the RSS (1925), the Bharatiya Jana Sangh (1951), and the BJP (1980); and the intellectual architects Shyama Prasad Mukherjee and Deendayal Upadhyaya.`,
            `RSS — The Ideological Womb (1925)`,
            `The Rashtriya Swayamsevak Sangh was founded on 27 September 1925 in Nagpur by Dr. Keshav Baliram Hedgewar, a physician and nationalist who was deeply influenced by Vinayak Damodar Savarkar's landmark 1923 text Hindutva: Who Is a Hindu? Hedgewar was frustrated by two realities: the Congress Party's pluralistic, secular nationalism which he felt diluted Hindu identity, and the growing communal tensions of the 1920s. His answer was not a political party but a cultural organisation — the RSS.`,
            `The RSS operated through the shakha, a daily neighbourhood gathering for physical exercise, nationalist songs, ideological discussion, and character formation. The shakha was the genius of RSS's model: it built a cadre of disciplined, selfless, ideologically aligned men at the grassroots, across every town and village in India. This grassroots network would later become BJP's unmatched electoral infrastructure.`,
            `Hedgewar's core thesis, absorbed from Savarkar, was Hindutva — not Hinduism as a religion, but 'Hindu-ness' as a civilisational identity encompassing all who regard India as both their fatherland and holy land. This distinction — between a religious category and a civilisational-political one — would become the ideological cornerstone of every organisation that followed.`,
            `After Hedgewar's death in 1940, the organisation's second Sarsanghchalak, Madhav Sadashiv Golwalkar (known as 'Guruji'), led the RSS for 33 years and expanded it significantly. Golwalkar consolidated the RSS as a purely cultural movement — explicitly avoiding party politics — but the 1948 crisis changed everything.`,
            `The 1948 Crisis and the Turn to Politics`,
            `On 30 January 1948, Mahatma Gandhi was assassinated by Nathuram Godse, an RSS member. The Congress government of Jawaharlal Nehru banned the RSS. Though the ban was lifted in 1949 after the RSS adopted a constitution swearing loyalty to the Indian Constitution, the crisis had revealed a fundamental vulnerability: without a voice in Parliament, the RSS and its ideology could be outlawed at any moment. Golwalkar, under pressure from his own cadres who had suffered arrest and prosecution, agreed to support the formation of a political organisation.`,
            `| 1925 | RSS founded by K.B. Hedgewar in Nagpur. Shakha model begins. |`,
            `| --- | --- |`,
            `| 1940 | Hedgewar dies. Golwalkar (Guruji) becomes Sarsanghchalak for 33 years. |`,
            `| 1948 | Gandhi assassination by RSS member Godse. RSS banned. Revoked 1949. |`,
            `| 1951 | Golwalkar backs formation of Bharatiya Jana Sangh under Mukherjee. |`,
            `| 1973 | Deoras succeeds Golwalkar. RSS pivots more actively toward politics. |`,
            `| 1975 | Emergency. RSS leaders jailed. Forged unity with opposition. |`,
            `| 1980 | Ex-Jana Sangh leaders form BJP. RSS provides cadre and ideological spine. |`,
            `Bharatiya Jana Sangh — The Political Arm (1951)`,
            `Shyama Prasad Mukherjee was, by any measure, one of the most formidable minds in post-independence India. Born on 6 July 1901 into a distinguished Bengal family — his father was Vice-Chancellor of Calcutta University and a judge — he became the youngest Vice-Chancellor of Calcutta University himself at 33. He was a barrister, an educationist, and a parliamentarian of the first order. Nehru, despite being his ideological opposite, respected him enough to bring him into independent India's first cabinet as Industry & Supply Minister.`,
            `The break came over the Liaquat-Nehru Pact of 1950, which sought to protect minorities in both India and Pakistan. Mukherjee considered it an appeasement of Pakistan that left Hindus in East Bengal unprotected. He resigned from the cabinet on 6 April 1950. Freed from government, he began building what would become the first organised political opposition to Nehruvian Congress.`,
            `After consulting Golwalkar and drawing on the RSS's extensive ground network, Mukherjee formally founded the Bharatiya Jana Sangh on 21 October 1951 in Delhi. The RSS supplied young, energetic pracharaks — full-time volunteers who had renounced personal life for national service — as the party's organisational backbone. Key among them was Deendayal Upadhyaya, who became the party's first General Secretary.`,
            `Jana Sangh's Founding Agenda`,
            `The Jana Sangh's core agenda was clear and unapologetic. It stood for:`,
            `- One Nation, One Constitution: Complete integration of Jammu & Kashmir and abrogation of Article 370, which Mukherjee called the 'Balkanisation of India.'`,
            `- Cultural nationalism: India defined by its Hindu civilisational identity, not by Nehruvian secularism.`,
            `- Anti-appeasement: 'Justice for all, appeasement of none' — opposing what they saw as Congress's vote-bank politics favouring minorities.`,
            `- Swadeshi economy: Indigenous enterprise, self-reliance, small industries over foreign capital.`,
            `- Strong national defence: Resistance to China and Pakistan, a muscular foreign policy.`,
            `<em>Ek desh<strong> </strong>mein<strong> </strong>do Vidhan, do Pradhan aur do Nishan nahi<strong> challenge </strong>— One country cannot have two constitutions, two prime ministers and two flags. — Shyama Prasad Mukherjee</em>`,
            `The Martyrdom of Mukherjee (1953)`,
            `In early 1953, Mukherjee launched a Satyagraha against the permit system that required Indian citizens to carry an ID card to enter Jammu & Kashmir — their own country. He entered the state without a permit on 11 May 1953 and was arrested under the J&K Public Safety Act. He died in detention in Srinagar on 23 June 1953 under circumstances his party called mysterious. He was 52.`,
            `The impact of his death cannot be overstated. It deprived the party of its only parliamentarian of national stature — but it gave the movement its first martyr. His death galvanised the Jana Sangh. Atal Bihari Vajpayee, then a young editor who had covered the Kashmir satyagraha, attributed his entry into politics directly to Mukherjee's death. Sixty-six years later, on 5 August 2019, BJP members would describe the abrogation of Article 370 by the Modi government as the fulfilment of Mukherjee's dying demand.`,
            `Deendayal Upadhyaya and Integral Humanism`,
            `With Mukherjee dead and the party barely two years old, its survival fell to Deendayal Upadhyaya — an RSS pracharak of deep intellectual capability who served as the party's General Secretary from 1951 to 1967. Upadhyaya was not a politician in the conventional sense. He was a philosopher-organiser who believed that a political party without a robust philosophical foundation was merely an electoral machine.`,
            `In 1965 he articulated Integral Humanism — a philosophical framework that he proposed as an authentically Indian alternative to both Western liberal capitalism and Soviet socialism. The framework rests on four human goals (Purusharthas) — Dharma (righteousness), Artha (material welfare), Kama (desire/fulfilment), and Moksha (liberation) — and insists that a just society must satisfy all four in harmony. It emphasised:`,
            `- Self-reliance over foreign dependence`,
            `- Decentralised governance rooted in village and community`,
            `- The concept of Chiti — the soul or identity of a civilisation — as the organising principle of national life`,
            `- Antyodaya — welfare of the last person in society, the most marginalised`,
            `- Rejection of class conflict in favour of social harmony`,
            `Integral Humanism remains, to this day, the official ideology of the BJP as inscribed in its constitution. When BJP leaders speak of 'Antyodaya' in the context of welfare schemes like PM Awas Yojana or Ujjwala, they are consciously invoking Upadhyaya's framework. Upadhyaya was elected President of the Jana Sangh in 1967. On 11 February 1968 he was found dead near Mughal Sarai railway station under unresolved circumstances. Once again, the movement had lost its most visionary leader at a critical moment.`,
            `Ideology Evolution — From Gandhian Socialism to Development Nationalism`,
            `One of the most intellectually interesting aspects of BJP's history is how its public ideological positioning has shifted across phases, even as the RSS core remained constant. These shifts were largely tactical responses to electoral realities, but they also reflected genuine learning and adaptation.`,
            `Phase 1 — Jana Sangh Era (1951–1977): Unapologetic Hindu nationalism, anti-Congress positioning, strong on One Nation and cultural identity. Electoral presence limited primarily to the Hindi belt.`,
            `Phase 2 — Early BJP (1980–1989): The newly formed BJP under Vajpayee initially moderated its Hindutva stance, adopting 'Gandhian Socialism' as its working philosophy to differentiate from the Jana Sangh's image and appeal to a broader electorate. This earned it only 2 seats in 1984 — its worst performance. The experiment was declared a failure.`,
            `Phase 3 — Advani Era (1989–1999): Return to assertive Hindu nationalism, culminating in the Ram Rath Yatra (1990), the Babri Masjid demolition (1992), and a surge to 182 seats in 1996. The NDA government under Vajpayee (1998-2004) balanced Hindutva with economic liberalisation — the 'India Shining' era.`,
            `Phase 4 — Modi Era (2014–present): Ideological synthesis. Hindutva cultural nationalism is fused with aggressive development economics, welfare delivery to the poor, strong foreign policy, and technological modernisation. This is sometimes called 'New Nationalism' or 'development nationalism' — a blend of Upadhyaya's Antyodaya and a muscular state. The result: two consecutive majorities in 2014 and 2019, and a continuing dominant position in 2024.`
          ]
